RUNNERS#AGAINST
I#ROME###ULNA#E
OF#WINE#AEON#HM
TAM#REVERSE#TAP
IRON#WAKES#PART
NOSES#DEN#OLIVE
G#TRIPE#ANNUL#R
###VMI###RIM###
P#ROPES#PROBE#L
EPEUS#TIE#NERVE
BEES#CORNS#RIOT
BAD#PARENTS#NIT
LL#PUNK#YALE#DE
E#SOLO###RARE#R
SETTLES#ESTATES


A1. Famous products of Finland. ~ RUNNERS
A6. In opposition to. ~ AGAINST
A12. All roads lead to this place. (Nothing to do with detour signs.) ~ ROME
A13. A doctor's funnybone; or one of the two long bones of the forearm. ~ ULNA
A14. Preposition usually between Prince and Wales. ~ OF
A16. A forbidden beverage in the Land of the Free. (What's funny about that sentence?) ~ WINE
A18. Classic form of "Eon." ~ AEON
A19. Heavy Mamma (abbr.). ~ HM
A20. Something a dumb Scotchman wears over the weak end. ~ TAM
A22. Kind of English often used on a cue ball. ~ REVERSE
A24. What we used to find beer on. ~ TAP
A25. This is sometimes flat and sometimes sad. ~ IRON
A27. Something the little woman always does when her husband comes home at 3 A.M. ~ WAKES
A28. A fool and his flivver soon do this. ~ PART
A29. What horses win by. ~ NOSES
A31. An author's factory. ~ DEN
A32. An Irish plum. ~ OLIVE
A33. A food; but not much. ~ TRIPE
A35. Render void. ~ ANNUL
A37. Veteran's Matrimony Insurance (abbr.) ~ VMI
A38. This is supposed to be demountable. (Don't ask the Prince of Wales.) ~ RIM
A40. These keep pugilists in the ring. ~ ROPES
A42. What Tea Pot Dome caused. ~ PROBE
A45. The contractor of the wooden horse left at Troy. ~ EPEUS
A47. What the race between motorists and express trains often ends in. (You have to give 'em credit, they come to their own conclusions!) ~ TIE
A49. What it takes to strike the boss for a raise. ~ NERVE
A51. Famous knees. ~ BEES
A52. Little things that great aches grow from. ~ CORNS
A54. An Irish picnic. ~ RIOT
A55. What Jesse James was. ~ BAD
A56. These are people that flappers don't pay any attention to. ~ PARENTS
A58. This is one way of saying "No!" ~ NIT
A59. Lavender Language (abbr.) ~ LL
A60. No good. ~ PUNK
A61. A brain (?) factory. ~ YALE
A63. A colored gentleman's definite article. ~ DE
A64. You don't have to be crazy to play this on a saxophone, but it helps a lot. ~ SOLO
A65. What an old-fashioned girl is and we don't mean maybe. ~ RARE
A67. It's claimed that "the woman pays" but it's the husband who does this. ~ SETTLES
A68. Millionaire's farms. ~ ESTATES

D1. Chief amusement in Herrin, Ill. ~ RIOTING
D2. Notorious Rummies (abbr.). ~ NR
D3. The correct time to take a drink; or what have you? ~ NOW
D4. The heir to the harem. ~ EMIR
D5. To renovate. ~ RENEW
D7. What weather men do for a living? ~ GUESS
D8. A plant used in the manufacture of a bitter medicine. ~ ALOE
D9. The lair of the modern highway robbers. ~ INN
D10. Neglected Antelopes (abbr.). ~ NA
D11. The snake in the Garden of Eden. ~ TEMPTER
D15. A wild and woolly game. ~ FARO
D17. What men would like to do when the time comes to pay their income tax. ~ EVADE
D18. This is in the middle of the Roman coliseum. ~ ARENA
D19. An abbreviation for one of Yale's friendly enemies. ~ HARV
D21. The number of married men who wish they were single. (Very conservative estimate!) ~ MOST
D23. The way most of us make a living. ~ EKE
D24. A horse's fly swatter. ~ TAIL
D26. The State in which the greatest number of people are married. ~ NERVOUS
D28. This kind of a man forgets everything but how to charge. ~ PLUMBER
D30. Dumb-bells. ~ SIMPS
D32. An apple a day keeps the doctor away; one of these a day keeps everyone away. ~ ONION
D34. The all-American dessert. ~ PIE
D36. Neurotic Railroads (abbr.). ~ NRR
D39. Sling shot ammunition (pl.). ~ PEBBLES
D40. The little thing that makes all the racket in a saxophone. ~ REED
D41. There's no closed season on this bird. ~ STORK
D42. A Scotch tip. ~ PENNY
D43. Home of policemen and shilalahs. ~ ERIN
D44. There are nine of these in Tennessee. ~ LETTERS
D46. Something a dumbbell couldn't do. ~ PEAL
D48. Crossword anger. ~ IRE
D50. A Hebrew "word"; also a useless contract. ~ VOID
D52. Scotchmen haven't much use for this because it tips so frequently. ~ CANOE
D53. You don't have to study astronomy to see these. (Ask any pedestrian!) ~ STARS
D56. What dentists get their job through. ~ PULL
D57. An important part of an old-fashioned bed. ~ SLAT
D60. A cooking vessel. ~ POT
D62. A series of years. ~ ERA
D64. Abbreviation for something cars run on. ~ ST
D66. And (Latin). ~ ET


